TASK 8 – Trace the Compilation Process (4 Marks)
1. Brief explanation
A C program passes through four main stages before it runs: preprocessing, compilation, assembly and linking. Each stage transforms the source code into a form that the next stage can use.
1. Source code

billing.c

2. Preprocessing — gcc -E

Expands macros and includes headers

3. Compilation — gcc -S

Generates assembly code

4. Assembly — gcc -c

Generates an object file

5. Linking

Produces the executable program

CODE:


#include <stdio.h>

#define FIXED_CHARGE_PAISE 5000L
#define RATE2_PAISE 225L
#define RATE3_PAISE 450L

long compute_bill(int units)
{
    long paise = FIXED_CHARGE_PAISE;

    if (units < 0)
        return -1;

    if (units > 100) {
        int slab2 = units < 200 ? units - 100 : 100;
        paise += (long)slab2 * RATE2_PAISE;
    }

    if (units > 200)
        paise += (long)(units - 200) * RATE3_PAISE;

#ifdef DEBUG
    printf("[DEBUG] Energy and fixed charges = %ld paise\n",
           paise);
#endif

    return paise;
}

int main(void)
{
    int units;

    printf("Enter units consumed: ");

    if (scanf("%d", &units) != 1 || units < 0) {
        printf("Invalid input!\n");
        return 1;
    }

    long bill = compute_bill(units);

    printf("Total bill: Rs. %ld.%02ld\n",
           bill / 100, bill % 100);

    return 0;
}

SAMPLE INPUT:
350

OUTPUT:

Enter units consumed: 350
Total bill: Rs. 950.00




